{"stream": "audit", "run_id": "res-0168b546d373", "event": "outbound_call", "ts": 1787741234.9734402, "service": "mcp", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8080/mcp", "method": "POST", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 3207.3, "attempt": 1, "error": "", "params": {"tool": "upsert_lead_profile", "employee_id": "E00010", "company_id": "C0010", "decision_maker_flag": "Yes", "lead_context": "Health Catalyst is a Nasdaq-listed healthcare data and analytics company whose core proposition sits squarely at the intersection of the post's theme. More than 1,100 organizations worldwide rely on Health Catalyst's cloud-based ecosystem, including its Ignite™ platform and AI-enabled analytics, to drive outcomes. The company has been publicly vocal about the very tension the post addresses: as healthcare leaders face unprecedented decisions around revenue, cost, and quality, they turn to AI to maximize analytics — yet they struggle to implement AI into existing workflows, demonstrate ROI, and move AI efforts beyond predictive models. Health Catalyst's own product philosophy reinforces this: health systems shouldn't attempt enterprise transformation all at once; their Ignite platform is modular so organizations can start small, prove value, and expand into broader clinical, financial, and operational domains. This maps directly onto the broader industry moment — from mid-2025 onwards, the message to healthcare IT and data leaders has been consistent: 2026 is not about proving that AI works, but proving it works at scale, under real operational pressure, with clear financial and clinical value. Health Catalyst also recently announced a strategic partnership with Microsoft, building on Azure and Azure AI Foundry to drive reliable insight and sustained outcomes for care providers. A strong opening question for the rep: When Health Catalyst's own clients reach the end of a successful AI pilot on Ignite, what does the internal governance and change-management process look like to move that pilot into enterprise-wide adoption?"}}
