{"stream": "audit", "run_id": "res-0168b546d373", "event": "outbound_call", "ts": 1787741254.777953, "service": "mcp", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8080/mcp", "method": "POST", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 2996.8, "attempt": 1, "error": "", "params": {"tool": "upsert_lead_profile", "employee_id": "E00013", "company_id": "C0013", "decision_maker_flag": "Yes", "lead_context": "Mahesh Venkatagiri holds a VP-level role at what was Accolade — now fully absorbed into Transcarent following a $621M acquisition completed in April 2025. The combined entity is no small integration project: it now serves over 20 million members and more than 1,700 employer and health plan clients. The strategic logic is explicitly AI-first — Transcarent's AI-powered WayFinding and specialized Care Experiences are being combined with Accolade's proven expertise in health advocacy, expert medical opinions, and virtual primary care. Operationally, this means the organization is in active mid-integration mode, pressure-testing how AI actually performs at enterprise scale rather than in controlled pilots. Their shared investment in AI has already produced tools like Accolade's True Health Actions, which generate personalized care plans using data from health records, employers, and health plans to guide member outreach and care coordination. This context aligns squarely with a post about successful AI pilots in healthcare: the Transcarent/Accolade organization has bet its entire post-merger identity on moving AI from pilot to production. As GE HealthCare's Chief Science Officer noted, \"provider AI spend increasingly moved from pilots to platforms\" in 2025. A strong opening question for a rep: **How is the team thinking about measuring the ROI of AI pilots that were scoped under the old Accolade structure, now that the platform is being unified under Transcarent's WayFinding model?**"}}
{"stream": "audit", "run_id": "res-ff3acbe0007e", "event": "outbound_call", "ts": 1787741259.2117097, "service": "mcp", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8080/mcp", "method": "POST", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 3756.9, "attempt": 1, "error": "", "params": {"tool": "upsert_lead_profile", "employee_id": "E00010", "company_id": "C0010", "decision_maker_flag": "Yes", "lead_context": "Health Catalyst is in the middle of a deliberate strategic reset squarely focused on AI. The company spent 2025 reshaping its leadership team, expanding strategic partnerships, and launching new AI-enabled products, including forging an AI partnership with Microsoft and acquiring patient engagement company Upfront Healthcare Services. Its flagship Ignite Intelligence platform combines shared models, AI agents, benchmarks, and comparison services, with Clinical Cost Intelligence moving healthcare costing from months of analysis to instant insights using ML-driven variation analysis and LLM-enabled opportunity screening. Health Catalyst has also committed to prioritizing the utilization of AI across its internal teams in 2026. This positions the company squarely inside the broader industry tension our post addresses: by the end of 2025, many healthcare organizations had dozens of AI pilots live yet only a small subset could demonstrate a measurable clinical or financial outcome, shifting the core question from \"how do we try AI?\" to \"how do we run AI as critical infrastructure?\" As a data-and-analytics vendor whose value proposition is built on measurable improvement for provider organizations, Health Catalyst is both subject to this pressure and selling into it. A strong opening question for a first conversation: **How is Health Catalyst helping its provider clients move their AI deployments from successful pilots into sustained, board-defensible outcomes at scale — and what does that transition look like internally for their own AI investments?**"}}
