{"stream": "audit", "run_id": "res-0168b546d373", "event": "outbound_call", "ts": 1787741279.700951, "service": "mcp", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8080/mcp", "method": "POST", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 2847.3, "attempt": 1, "error": "", "params": {"tool": "upsert_lead_profile", "employee_id": "E00016", "company_id": "C0016", "decision_maker_flag": "Yes", "lead_context": "Innovaccer is not a prospect still evaluating whether AI works in healthcare — it is a vendor positioned at the center of the industry's current challenge: moving customers from isolated pilots to enterprise-wide AI execution. Ranked #1 by Black Book Research in both AI-Powered Revenue Cycle Autonomy and Population Health Data Integration in 2026, Innovaccer's platform has been recognized for real-world execution, operational fit, and measurable impact — precisely the outcomes the post addresses. At its Xccelerate 2026 conference, Innovaccer convened health system and payer executives grappling with rising care demand, constrained workforce supply, tightening margins, and growing administrative complexity. The core question for healthcare technology leaders has shifted from \"How do we try AI?\" to \"How do we run AI as critical infrastructure?\" — a tension that lives inside Innovaccer's own customer base. Research shows 43% of health system executives are piloting or testing agentic AI, while only 3% have agents running in live workflows, meaning Innovaccer's clients face exactly the pilot-to-production gap the post targets. As Managing Director, Naveen likely owns or influences how Innovaccer packages and communicates AI deployment success to prospects and customers. A strong opening question: *When Innovaccer's customers move from a successful pilot to full deployment, what is the single biggest barrier they hit — and how are you helping them get past it?*"}}
{"stream": "audit", "run_id": "res-ff3acbe0007e", "event": "outbound_call", "ts": 1787741282.5354629, "service": "mcp", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8080/mcp", "method": "POST", "status": 200, "duration_ms": 4730.2, "attempt": 1, "error": "", "params": {"tool": "upsert_lead_profile", "employee_id": "E00013", "company_id": "C0013", "decision_maker_flag": "Yes", "lead_context": "Accolade was acquired by Transcarent in April 2025 in a $621 million deal, and the combined organization — now fully private — serves over 20 million members and more than 1,700 employer and health plan clients. The integration is explicitly AI-first: by blending Transcarent's AI-powered WayFinding navigation system with Accolade's True Health Actions platform, the combined entity provides personalized care guidance drawn from employer data, health plans, and electronic health records. Engineers from both companies have been building generative AI tools that enhance clinician productivity by reducing administrative burdens such as billing and prescriptions. This makes the post's theme — what a successful AI pilot looks like in healthcare — directly germane: the combined Transcarent/Accolade organization is in the thick of proving out exactly that, at scale, across a large self-insured employer client base. The broader industry pressure reinforces urgency: \"In 2026 we will see AI shift from isolated pilot programs to full enterprise-scale deployment, driven by clearer return on investment.\" For a VP at this organization, the practical challenge is no longer whether to deploy AI but how to demonstrate that pilots actually hold up when embedded in core member-navigation workflows. A strong opening question for the rep: *As Transcarent and Accolade finish integrating their platforms, what does \"success\" look like for an AI deployment at your scale — and how are you measuring it beyond the pilot stage?*"}}
